-### [Rasterization-based Voxelization](908-rasterization-based-voxelization.md)
-Converts a continuous triangle mesh into a discrete volumetric representation by projecting the geometry onto a 3D grid using rasterization principles. It solves the problem of generating surface-only (shell) voxel data or explicit cube meshes from polygonal input without performing complex surface reconstruction like QEF solving. It differs from [Analytic Voxelization](10493-analytic-voxelization.md) by relying on discrete sampling and rasterization logic rather than exact geometric intersection tests, and from [Voxel Carving](1213-voxel-carving.md) by projecting geometry into the volume rather than subtracting from a solid block [G].
-
 ### [Point Cloud Voxelization](1000-point-cloud-voxelization.md)
-Converts an unstructured set of 3D points into a discrete volumetric grid representation suitable for rendering or processing. It solves the problem of mapping continuous spatial coordinates to a fixed-size array by assigning each point to a specific voxel cell based on its position relative to a defined bounding box and voxel size. Unlike [Hard Voxelization](5345-hard-voxelization.md), which typically focuses on simple occupancy, this method often aggregates point features (such as color or intensity) into the voxel cells, providing a dense or sparse structured output. It differs from [Ray Model Voxelization](4688-ray-model-voxelization.md) by not explicitly modeling free space between the sensor and points, and from [Voxels to Points Projection](3172-voxels-to-points-projection.md) by aggregating data into the grid rather than projecting it back.
+Converts an unstructured set of 3D points into a discrete volumetric grid representation by mapping each point to a specific voxel index based on spatial coordinates. Chosen when transforming sparse, irregular point data into a regular grid is required.
 
 ### [VoxelGrid from Mesh (Fill Method)](1013-voxelgrid-from-mesh-fill-method.md)
-The Fill Method generates a solid voxel grid from a mesh by computing surface occupancy and then applying binary morphological hole filling. It builds on [Voxelization](12-voxelization.md) by using a two-step surface-then-fill approach rather than direct volume rasterization or geometric intersection tests.
+Converts a triangular mesh into a dense voxel grid by first computing surface occupancy and then filling internal voids using morphological operations. Chosen when a solid, watertight representation is needed rather than a surface shell.
 
 ### [Voxel Grid Data Generation Pipeline](1208-voxel-grid-data-generation-pipeline.md)
-This method generates labeled 3D voxel grids from sequential scan data, specifically designed to handle occlusions and temporal consistency between prior and current observations. It differs from standard voxelization by maintaining two distinct grid states (prior and past) and explicitly updating occlusion masks based on camera pose transformations, ensuring that newly observed geometry correctly occludes previous estimates. It builds on [Voxelization](12-voxelization.md) by adding a temporal, scan-based processing loop [G].
-
-### [Voxel Carving](1213-voxel-carving.md)
-Voxel Carving is a technique that reconstructs a dense, filled 3D volumetric representation from a set of 2D depth maps or silhouettes captured from multiple viewpoints. Unlike surface-only voxelization methods that populate only the boundary voxels, Voxel Carving fills the interior volume consistent with the input views, creating a solid model of the given shape. It is distinguished from sibling methods like [Rasterization-based Voxelization](908-rasterization-based-voxelization.md) by its reliance on multi-view geometric consistency (silhouette intersection or depth truncation) rather than single-view rasterization or point cloud projection.
