-### Voxelization for Boolean Operations
-A technique to voxelize surface meshes for volume rendering and boolean operations to create occlusion-free multi-field visualizations [S13, unofficial]. It is used to perform binary operations on sets of single-field features in multi-field visualization [S13, unofficial].
+### Voxel Downsampling with Material Propagation
+Determines if a coarse voxel is solid by checking its 8 children; if at least 4 of the 8 children are solid, the coarse voxel is solid [S11, unofficial]. Determines the material of a coarse voxel by taking the most common solid child Material [S11, unofficial]. Edits propagate up all LOD levels immediately, ensuring features like craters are visible from orbit [S11, unofficial].
 
-### Surface Voxel Texture Update
-If `surface_voxels` is None, call `sbs.create_surface_voxel_structure(volume_data)` to generate them [S14, unofficial]. If `surface_voxel_texture` does not exist, generate it and set min/mag filter to `GL_LINEAR`, wrap S/T to `GL_CLAMP_TO_EDGE` [S14, unofficial]. Convert `surface_voxels` list to numpy array of shape $(N, 7)$ where each row is $(x, y, z, nx, ny, nz, ?)$ cast to float32. Extract first 6 components $(x,y,z,nx,ny,nz)$ for texture data [S14, unofficial]. Limit texture width to $\min(\text{surface\_data.shape[0]}, \text{GL\_MAX\_TEXTURE\_SIZE})$. Upload as 2D texture with width=limit, height=1, internal format `GL_RGBA32F`, format `GL_RGBA`, type `GL_FLOAT` [S14, unofficial].
+### Porosity via Spatial Pseudo-Random Hash Drop-out
+A technique to achieve desired porosity levels in voxelization by dropping out voxels using a spatial pseudo-random hash function [S12, unofficial]. The `porosity` parameter controls the desired level, passed to `MeshProcessor::voxelize` and exposed via a CLI argument `--porosity <FLOAT>` [S12, unofficial].
+
+### Block-Wise Reconstruction
+Reconstructs a cubic block of voxels at once using a three-pass 3D wavelet reconstruction process, rather than reconstructing each voxel individually, eliminating redundancy [S13, unofficial]. Applies a 1D wavelet reconstruction along the x dimension first, then y, then z to reconstruct the final block [S13, unofficial]. Computational cost for one pass is $mn^3$; total cost for three passes is $3mn^3$ [S13, unofficial].
+
+### Voxelization of Delaunay Cover
+Initializes a zero matrix $V$ and sets the corresponding voxel index in $V$ to 1 for each point $p_i$ in $P_\kappa$ [S14, unofficial]. Classical 3D matrix representation grows as a cube of cells, causing memory issues; the solution is to use a sparse hash table storing only nonzero entries [S14, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Voxelization is a fundamental technique in computer graphics and scientific visualization, serving as the bridge between continuous geometric representations and discrete volumetric data structures. Its origins lie in the need to render and process 3D data on raster displays and later, on GPU architectures. Early methods focused on scan-converting polygonal meshes into 2D slices or 3D grids for volume rendering and collision detection [S1]. The advent of GPU computing enabled real-time voxelization techniques, such as rasterization-based approaches and GPU-accelerated point cloud voxelization, which are now standard in fields like medical imaging, 3D printing, and real-time global illumination (e.g., Voxel Cone Tracing) [S3, S5, unofficial]. Modern developments include differentiable voxelization for neural rendering and hierarchical sparse structures for large-scale scene management [S11, S4391].
